From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] ui/cocoa: Omarchy's logo instead of "Display output is not active."

What: until the guest shows a picture on an output, and when it switches the
output off again (a reboot), the window shows Omarchy's logo, green on black,
where the firmware draws it, instead of QEMU's placeholder text ("Display
output is not active.", "Guest has not initialized the display (yet).").
Also on the windows of the other displays. If the guest has shown nothing of
its own 90 s of running time after it started (OMACVM_SPLASH_HINT_SECONDS), a
line under the logo says Omarchy is taking long and where its logs are
(OMACVM_LOGS, set by the launcher), and qemu.log gets a warning.
OMACVM_BOOT_SPLASH=0 brings QEMU's text back. The main window's layer now
draws again when the window changes size.

Why: every start showed QEMU's text for a few seconds (before the firmware,
and from GRUB until Linux draws), and every reboot for longer. It looked like
an error but is just the guest getting ready.

How: drawn in the window's GL layers, so UTM and the guest are not involved.
The logo is the firmware's (boot-logo/make-logo-bmp.py logo.svg --rows, from
Omarchy's logo.svg, MIT): an 81 x 19 texture with sharp cells, placed where
the firmware's stretched frame has its logo (1920 x 1080 on a cold start,
after a reboot the size of the guest's last picture, as virtio-gpu then
reports), so the hand-over to the firmware does not move it.

Upstream: OmacVM only (branding), not for QEMU.

Test: Tests/display/test-boot-splash-cells.py (the build runs it): the cells
in ui/cocoa.m make exactly the firmware's Logo.bmp of edk2-logo-omarchy.patch.
The start, the hand-over and a reboot were checked with window screenshots
(tracks/boot-splash in the work notes).

